title:    Disable the key, and the secret stops being readable
subtitle: measure-cmek-revocation.py — same identity, same permissions, secret untouched
width:    980
---
$ python scripts/measure-cmek-revocation.py --timeout 900 --poll 20

[05:13:22] control: secret IS readable with the key enabled
[05:13:27] key version 1 disabled (state=DISABLED); polling the read every 20s
[05:13:29] +   2s  read STILL SUCCEEDS
[05:13:52] +  25s  read STILL SUCCEEDS
[05:14:14] +  47s  read STILL SUCCEEDS
[05:14:37] +  70s  read DENIED
    ERROR: (gcloud.secrets.versions.access) FAILED_PRECONDITION:
    Failed precondition on Cloud KMS resource
    [.../keyRings/wk12-secrets/cryptoKeys/wk12-app-db-password]

[05:14:39] key version re-enabled (state=ENABLED)
[05:14:59] secret readable again: True

# THREE TRIALS, because one is not a measurement: denied after 70s, 2s, 27s.
#
# The SPREAD is the finding, not the number. A disable reaches Secret Manager
# somewhere between near-instantly and over a minute, so any fixed wait under
# ~70s will sometimes report a working control as broken. The first version of
# this test slept 30 seconds, read once, saw it succeed, and concluded the
# control did not exist.
#
# FAILED_PRECONDITION, not PERMISSION_DENIED. The caller still holds
# secretmanager.versions.access and the secret's own IAM policy was never
# touched. Nothing about WHO is asking changed - only whether Secret Manager
# can still decrypt, which it does as its own service agent using a key it no
# longer may use.
#
# Disable is not destroy: re-enabling restored the read. And a DISABLED key
# version is still a BILLED key version.
